# Append-only change streams (ADR-0014, ADR-0015). Records are only ever added
# at the end, so a merge should keep both sides' records instead of conflicting.
# `union` is a built-in git strategy: unlike a custom merge driver it needs no
# per-clone `git config` registration, so it also applies in CI, fresh clones,
# and worktrees. `bun run change-stream:guard` checks what union cannot —
# valid JSONL, unique ids, and no newly introduced timestamp inversions.
.skillset/changes/*.jsonl merge=union

# Generated output must never line-merge (ADR-0014 reasoning, SET-601).
#
# A half-merged generated file is the dangerous artifact in this system: it
# looks valid, it stages like a valid file, and it corresponds to no source.
# Only a regeneration should ever produce one. `-merge` makes git take the
# current branch's version whole and still declare the path conflicted, so the
# conflict survives as a signal while the markers never appear. Verified
# against git 2.55.0. It leaves `diff` untouched, unlike `binary`.
#
# Like `merge=union` above, `-merge` is built in: unlike a custom merge driver
# it needs no per-clone `git config` registration, so it also applies in CI,
# fresh clones, and worktrees. `skillset resolve` then repairs the conflicted
# paths by regenerating them, which is the only thing that can produce a valid
# generated file.
#
# `bun run generated-merge-policy:guard` checks what these globs cannot. It
# sorts every tracked file into one of three categories — generated snapshot,
# append-only ledger, authored — and requires each to resolve the one merge
# policy its category allows. It enumerates nothing: the generated set comes
# from the committed locks, the ledger set from the change-stream guard's own
# pathspec, and authored is the remainder. So these patterns only have to keep
# up with the locks, and an over-broad one is caught by the authored side.
#
# `**/` prefixes cover nested workspaces such as examples/first-author/. The
# generated plugin bundle root is anchored with a leading slash instead, because
# an unanchored `plugins/` also matches the authored `.skillset/plugins/` source
# tree — the guard below caught exactly that. It is `/plugins/*/**` rather than
# `/plugins/**` so it covers each generated bundle without claiming the authored
# `plugins/README.md` beside them; the guard caught that one too.
**/.agents/plugins/** -merge
**/.agents/skills/** -merge
**/.claude/rules/** -merge
**/.claude/settings.json -merge
**/.claude/skills/** -merge
**/.codex/hooks/** -merge
**/.cursor/rules/** -merge
**/.cursor/skills/** -merge
/plugins/*/** -merge
**/skillset.lock -merge
.skillset/plugins/*/CHANGELOG.md -merge

# Directory-local AGENTS.md files are generated from <source-root>/rules/**.
# The repository's own root AGENTS.md is authored, so it merges normally.
#
# `core.ignorecase` is true on a case-insensitive filesystem such as APFS, and
# git matches attributes case-insensitively when it is. So `**/AGENTS.md` also
# matches an authored `agents.md`, and does so on macOS but not on Linux CI. The
# docs tree is excluded below for that reason; it is owned by scripts/docs.ts
# and checked by `bun run docs:check`, so Skillset's merge policy does not apply
# there. Excluding it is safe because the guard checks the other direction too:
# a lock-claimed file under docs/ would fail as a generated path that merges.
**/AGENTS.md -merge
/AGENTS.md !merge
/docs/** !merge
